The '''Misión Los Ánxeles de Kuarupíba''' ({{Literal translation|Mission of the Angels of [[Kuarupíba]]}}) is a historic [[Levantine Catholic Church|Levantine Catholic]] [[W:Basilicas in the Catholic Church|basilica]] complex in the [[Los Ánxeles]] barrio of [[Ampurias]], within the [[Castejón|Castejón Metropolis]]. It was dedicated to Christians that were martyred during the [[Andalusi–Mauretanian wars]], and prior to the [[Baliscan Revolution]], it was operated by the [[Diocese of the Alpujarras]]. The mission was founded in the 17th century by missionaries of the [[Levantine Brotherhood|''Ermandad Libánti'']] ("Levantine Brotherhood", known as the ''Germanía'' in [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]]).

Within the [[Alpujarra]], the Levantines established eight reductions, collectively often called the [[Alpujarran missions]]. By 1641, despite occasional skirmishes with Burawi settlers and repeated epidemics, the Kuarupíba population of the Alpujarran missions had a population of 16,190. Over the next century, the mission population increased to a maximum of 87,562 in 1732. Populations of individual reductions varied from 500 to 5,000.

The immediate need of both the settlers the indigenous peoples in the 1640s was to protect themselves from rival colonists and authorities. The Levantines began to arm them, producing [[W:Musket|muskets]] and [[W:Gunpowder|gunpowder]] in the missions. They also secured the support of the [[Hesperian League|Hesperians]], and some arms, to raise militias of Indigenous people to defend the Alpujarra against raids. The militias would eventually number as many as 4,000 troops and their cavalry was especially effective, wearing Mesogean-style uniforms and carrying bows and arrows as well as muskets.

